//===- corpus_model.cpp - Deterministic corpus management regression model ===//
//
// This file is a *test*, not production code.  The objects under test are the
// production fuzzer::Merger (FuzzerMerge.cpp) and fuzzer::InputCorpus
// (FuzzerCorpus.h).  Their implementations are never copied: every behaviour
// is driven through the real classes and cross-checked against an independent
// reference model written from the documented control-log grammar and the
// documented ownership rules.
//
// Scope
// -----
// The inputs are finite, locally generated byte strings plus human-assigned
// feature and PC numbers.  No real fuzz target is fuzzed, no mutation engine
// runs, no process is crashed or killed.
//
// Two sides of corpus management share one replayable harness:
//   * offline merge: control-log projection (STARTED/FT/COV) plus the greedy
//     Merger::Merge selection;
//   * online shrinking: Fuzzer::RunOne ordering (AddFeature for every feature,
//     then AddToCorpus once) replayed against a real InputCorpus.
//
// Invariants that must hold on BOTH sides
// ---------------------------------------
//   1. A feature has at most one current owner.  Offline, ownership is the
//      union of features accumulated by the greedy pass; online, it is the
//      smallest-size input recorded per feature slot.
//   2. Selection is driven by feature *sets*, never by input counts alone.
//   3. Equal-size ties never transfer ownership (online AddFeature requires a
//      strictly smaller size; the merge comparator only orders, it never
//      grants coverage), so any legal ordering of an exact tie is acceptable.
//      Comparisons therefore use set equality; exact name ordering is only
//      asserted in tie-free cases.
//
// Outputs that must NOT be equated with each other
// -------------------------------------------------
//   * An FT line lists only the features that were previously unseen for the
//      whole merge run; it is not the full coverage of that input.  It must
//      never be compared one-to-one against an online InputInfo feature set.
//      Shared-feature takeovers are therefore constructed from separate
//      synthetic records.
//   * New PC (COV) statistics and file selection answer different questions:
//      a candidate can contribute new PCs and still be rejected because it
//      brings no new feature.
//   * NumFeatures() counts distinct feature slots ever created (monotonic);
//      NumFeatureUpdates() counts ownership changes.  Evictions decrement an
//      InputInfo's NumFeatures but never decrement NumFeatures().
//   * Merger control-file records and InputCorpus InputInfo entries use
//      different identity spaces (file paths vs corpus indices / SHA1); only
//      their set-level consequences are comparable.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

